WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.754
What if reaching a goal were just the reverse of a
diffusion process?

00:00:04.834 --> 00:00:06.261
In this ICML 2024 paper,

00:00:06.341 --> 00:00:11.976
Vineet Jain and Siamak Ravanbakhsh from McGill
University and Mila reframe goal-conditioned

00:00:12.056 --> 00:00:15.933
reinforcement learning through the lens of
denoising diffusion.

00:00:16.013 --> 00:00:17.691
Their method, called Merlin,

00:00:17.771 --> 00:00:23.972
builds trajectories that drift away from goal
states and then trains a policy to reverse that drift,

00:00:24.052 --> 00:00:27.677
exactly as a diffusion model denoises noise back
into data.

00:00:27.757 --> 00:00:33.644
The result is a value-function-free approach that
reaches goals from arbitrary starting states,

00:00:33.724 --> 00:00:37.161
needs only a single denoising step per environment step,

00:00:37.241 --> 00:00:43.190
and runs an order of magnitude faster than other
diffusion-based reinforcement learning methods.

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:49.012
Goal-conditioned reinforcement learning aims to
train a single agent that can reach any

00:00:49.092 --> 00:00:51.234
target state within an environment,

00:00:51.314 --> 00:00:56.185
using only a sparse reward of one when the goal is
reached and zero otherwise.

00:00:56.265 --> 00:01:01.327
In the offline setting, the agent must learn purely
from a pre-collected dataset,

00:01:01.407 --> 00:01:03.358
without any further interaction.

00:01:03.438 --> 00:01:07.738
The trouble is that most methods rely on estimating
a value function,

00:01:07.818 --> 00:01:12.118
and in the offline goal-conditioned setting this
estimate is fragile.

00:01:12.198 --> 00:01:15.292
Policies generate actions not present in the data,

00:01:15.372 --> 00:01:18.339
the value estimates for those actions are wrong,

00:01:18.419 --> 00:01:22.274
and these errors compound over time until the
policy diverges.

00:01:22.354 --> 00:01:26.210
Sparse binary rewards only make the estimation
problem harder.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:30.659
Diffusion models have become a powerful class of
generative models.

00:01:30.739 --> 00:01:36.169
They work by defining a forward process that
gradually destroys data into Gaussian noise,

00:01:36.249 --> 00:01:41.123
then learning a reverse process that denoises noise
back into realistic samples,

00:01:41.203 --> 00:01:43.662
without ever estimating a value function.

00:01:43.742 --> 00:01:50.225
The authors ask a simple question: what if we treat
goal states as the data distribution we want to model?

00:01:50.305 --> 00:01:54.064
In a diffusion model, noise walks away from the
data manifold;

00:01:54.144 --> 00:01:56.727
in goal-conditioned reinforcement learning,

00:01:56.807 --> 00:02:00.813
we can construct trajectories that walk away from
potential goals.

00:02:00.893 --> 00:02:06.386
Learning to reverse those deviations is directly
analogous to learning the score function,

00:02:06.466 --> 00:02:11.030
and it sidesteps the value-estimation problems that
plague offline methods.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:14.842
The paper makes three main contributions.

00:02:14.922 --> 00:02:18.353
First, it presents Merlin,

00:02:18.433 --> 00:02:21.865
a fresh perspective that casts goal-conditioned
reinforcement learning as a reverse diffusion

00:02:21.945 --> 00:02:25.377
process operating directly over the state space of
the environment.

00:02:25.457 --> 00:02:28.888
Second,

00:02:28.968 --> 00:02:32.400
it proves that this reverse process can be learned
by simple goal-conditioned behavior

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:35.912
cloning with hindsight relabeling,

00:02:35.992 --> 00:02:39.423
eliminating the need for a value function entirely.

00:02:39.503 --> 00:02:42.935
Third, it develops three ways to construct the forward,

00:02:43.015 --> 00:02:46.447
goal-departing trajectories: a fixed heuristic version,

00:02:46.527 --> 00:02:49.958
a parametric learned forward model called Merlin-P,

00:02:50.038 --> 00:02:53.470
and a non-parametric version called Merlin-NP that
stitches together nearby states in a learned latent space.

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:55.319
Here is how Merlin works.

00:02:55.399 --> 00:02:59.346
Take a trajectory from the offline dataset and read
it backwards,

00:02:59.426 --> 00:03:01.515
starting from its final goal state.

00:03:01.595 --> 00:03:08.764
Applying a forward diffusion transformation at each
step produces states that drift progressively away from the goal,

00:03:08.844 --> 00:03:12.110
exactly like adding noise in an image diffusion model.

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:17.129
Merlin then trains a goal-conditioned policy to
reverse this drift, step by step,

00:03:17.209 --> 00:03:18.430
back toward the goal.

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:23.697
The key theoretical result is that maximizing the
likelihood of the goal states under

00:03:23.777 --> 00:03:28.840
this reverse process is equivalent to plain
behavior cloning on the relabeled data,

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:31.070
so no value function is ever needed.

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:34.416
Because the state space itself is the diffusion space,

00:03:34.496 --> 00:03:38.877
the policy needs only one denoising iteration for
each environment step,

00:03:38.957 --> 00:03:42.967
instead of the many iterations typical diffusion
policies require.

00:03:43.047 --> 00:03:48.667
The authors offer three ways to build the
goal-departing trajectories: a fixed construction,

00:03:48.747 --> 00:03:51.579
a learned parametric forward model in Merlin-P,

00:03:51.659 --> 00:03:57.279
and a non-parametric model in Merlin-NP that
connects nearby states through nearest-neighbor

00:03:57.359 --> 00:03:59.510
stitching in a learned latent space.

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:06.181
The authors evaluate Merlin on a standard offline
goal-conditioned benchmark of ten control tasks,

00:04:06.261 --> 00:04:12.877
ranging from simple point navigation and reacher
tasks to harder Fetch manipulation tasks like pushing,

00:04:12.957 --> 00:04:16.972
picking, and sliding, plus the high-dimensional
HandReach task.

00:04:17.052 --> 00:04:22.563
Every task uses a sparse binary reward and a
maximum trajectory length of fifty steps.

00:04:22.643 --> 00:04:29.779
Each task comes in two flavors: an expert dataset
collected by a trained policy with added noise for diversity,

00:04:29.859 --> 00:04:33.549
and a random dataset collected by sampling random actions.

00:04:33.629 --> 00:04:39.400
They test both low-dimensional state observations
and high-dimensional pixel observations,

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:42.390
and average all results over ten random seeds.

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:45.283
The results are strong across the board.

00:04:45.363 --> 00:04:50.210
The basic version of Merlin already outperforms the
baselines on most tasks,

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:53.711
and the two improved variants, Merlin-P and Merlin-NP,

00:04:53.791 --> 00:04:58.963
push performance further to achieve the highest
discounted returns on most tasks.

00:04:59.043 --> 00:05:02.982
Measured by average rank across all ten methods and
ten tasks,

00:05:03.062 --> 00:05:04.733
Merlin-NP comes out on top,

00:05:04.813 --> 00:05:12.318
with an average rank around one point seven on
state observations and about one point two five on pixel observations.

00:05:12.398 --> 00:05:17.828
Crucially, because Merlin performs only a single
denoising step per environment step,

00:05:17.908 --> 00:05:24.182
its training and inference are roughly an order of
magnitude faster than the other diffusion-based

00:05:24.262 --> 00:05:26.775
methods like Decision Diffuser and BESO.

00:05:26.855 --> 00:05:29.433
With high-dimensional pixel observations,

00:05:29.513 --> 00:05:32.610
that efficiency gap becomes even more pronounced.

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:39.838
The authors study two key hyperparameters: the
hindsight relabeling ratio and the evaluation time horizon,

00:05:39.918 --> 00:05:42.518
which sets how far ahead the policy aims.

00:05:42.598 --> 00:05:47.617
Across all tasks, conditioning on a time horizon
clearly beats leaving it out.

00:05:47.697 --> 00:05:55.133
The best value depends on the task: easier tasks
like PointReach do well with a short horizon of one or five steps,

00:05:55.213 --> 00:06:01.343
while harder tasks benefit from longer horizons
because they need more steps to reach the goal.

00:06:01.423 --> 00:06:04.284
The HandReach task is particularly sensitive,

00:06:04.364 --> 00:06:09.448
working dramatically better with a horizon of one
than with any longer setting.

00:06:09.528 --> 00:06:16.180
These trends are visualized as heatmaps of returns
across horizons for both expert and random datasets.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:21.770
The big takeaway is that goal-conditioned
reinforcement learning can be reframed as the

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:23.655
reverse of a diffusion process,

00:06:23.735 --> 00:06:27.303
and that this reframing makes the problem
remarkably simple.

00:06:27.383 --> 00:06:32.411
By constructing trajectories that walk away from
goals and learning to reverse them,

00:06:32.491 --> 00:06:35.755
Merlin reduces goal-reaching to plain behavior cloning,

00:06:35.835 --> 00:06:39.585
with no value function to estimate and no
instability to fight.

00:06:39.665 --> 00:06:43.355
It matches or beats state-of-the-art methods across
ten tasks,

00:06:43.435 --> 00:06:47.976
yet runs an order of magnitude faster than other
diffusion-based approaches.

00:06:48.056 --> 00:06:52.233
This suggests that diffusion in the state space is
a simple, scalable,

00:06:52.313 --> 00:06:55.820
and practical new direction for sequential decision
making.
